<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Taifa of Badajoz</h1><p class="st-meta">1145 CE – 1150 CE (approx.) · capital Badajoz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Badajoz under the Banu Wazir family, one of the western regimes that arose when Almoravid rule broke down in the 1140s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1145 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-wazir">Banu Wazir</a>, the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidray-ibn-wazir">Sidray ibn Wazir</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-beja-and-evora">Taifa of Beja and Évora</a>, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> as part of the revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> that had begun in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known only from brief chronicle references, the state was a frontier territory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> facing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León</a>, governed by a military family that switched alliances as needed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By 1150 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-wazir">Banu Wazir</a> had submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, who kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> as a frontier stronghold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> took the city in 1230.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1145 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.49%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1145 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Badajoz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Badajoz of Ibn Wazir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1148&amp;focus=second-taifa-of-badajoz" aria-label="Second Taifa of Badajoz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4507 783 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Taifa of Badajoz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4827 1039L4799 1289M4827 1039L4794 1282"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4799 1289h0M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4827 1039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4827 1039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Badajoz. <a href="/atlas?year=1148&amp;focus=second-taifa-of-badajoz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
